## 3D Representation
- Voxels (Volumetric Elements)
- Point Cloud: (x,y,z) coordinate + attributes such as colour, normal, …
- (Polygonal) Meshes: Vertices + Faces forming triangles
	- Standard 3D Representation
- Parametric Surfaces: Splines, NURBS
- Implicit Surfaces: Signed Distance Field → Truncated SDF
	- Important for 3D Scanning
## Obtaining 3D
- 3D Modelling (e.g. Blender)
- Medical Imaging (e.g. MRI, CT)
- Depth Sensors: Depth Camera is based on stereo vision, using Triangulation
## Capture Devices
### Passive
- Stereo Depth Camera
	- two fixed RGB cameras take picture at same time
	- rolling shutter: capture pixels row by row → potentially distorted
	- global shutter: small number of pixels, capture everything all at once
	- Stereo Matching: finding correspondence (using triangulation / epipolar geometry)
	- Sum of Squared Differences (SSD) to find best possible match
	- More distance between cameras → more difficulties to calibrate but better result (numeric stability)
	- Sparse Stereo Matching: Find and match “interesting” points (SIFT, SUFF, ORB)
	- Search Window: too small → more noise, too big → less details
	- Deep Learning / CNN can be used for feature matching
- Multi-View Stereo
	- e.g. Dome of Cameras

### Active
- Active Stereo
	- project random pattern using Infrared (IR) to help reconstruct 3D scene
	- usage of a Beam Splitter: one lens with IR and RGB sensor
- Structured Light
	- replace one of the stereo cameras with a projector
	- IR image → estimate Depth → compute Normals from Depth
	- Laser Camera: one camera replaced with laser camera
	- high quality result / precision (depending on with of laser line)
- Time of Flight (ToF)
	- measure time between sending and receiving light sensor
	- useful for large objects / distances
	- not precise for small objects / distances → much noise
	- LIDAR = Light Imaging Detection and Ranging
	- used by autonomous driving cars, useful for large distances using GPS
## Camera
